% The blank line 2071 closes the source envelope; Corollary I.10.3 begins at
% line 2072 and is excluded.
% Corrected TeX and both checked print witnesses attest X in the term being
% defined. The criterion and following corollary instead use Y.

An algebra \(L\) of finite rank over~\(K\) will be said to be
\emph{unramified over~\(X\)} (or simply unramified over~\(K\), if \(X\) is
understood) if \(L\) is a separable algebra over~\(K\), that is, a direct
product of separable extensions \(K_i\), and if the normalization \(Y'\)
of~\(Y\) in~\(L\) (the disjoint union of the normalizations of~\(Y\) in
the \(K_i\)) is unramified (that is, étale by
Corollary~\ref{I.9.11}) over~\(Y\). Thus:

\paragraph{Editorial note.}
The corrected French authority TeX, the original printing, and the corrected
re-typeset witness all read \(X\) in the term being defined and in the phrase
``if \(X\) is understood.'' The defining criterion, the identity
\(K=R(Y)\), and \hyperref[I.10.3]{Corollary~I.10.3} instead use \(Y\). Thus \(Y\) appears
intended in those two places. We retain the attested \(X\) reading and flag
the apparent source oddity.
